\section{Writing your own builder}{


Every \verb{er_style_tte_*()} function above shares the signature documented
in the "Arguments" section above, and that signature is a public part of
the API: any function \verb{function(data, config, stratify, time, strata, theme, ...)} that returns a geom or list of geoms can stand in for a
built-in builder, passed as \code{style} to the matching \verb{er_tte_add_*()}
function.

A custom builder can self-declare which layer it's meant for via
\code{er_style_tag(builder, layer = ...)}, one of \code{"curve"}, \code{"censor"},
\code{"risktable"}, \code{"tte_summary"}, or \code{"tte_model"}. Every \verb{er_tte_add_*()}
function checks a builder's \code{layer} tag, if it has one, against the
layer it was actually passed to, erroring immediately if they disagree.
\code{"tte_summary"}/\code{"tte_model"} are deliberately distinct from
\code{\link[=er_plot_add_summary]{er_plot_add_summary()}}/\code{\link[=er_plot_add_model]{er_plot_add_model()}}'s own \code{"summary"}/\code{"model"}
tags -- the two grammars' summary/model builders share no signature or
\code{config} contents, so a builder written for \code{er_plot()} would otherwise
silently pass the tag check if passed to \code{er_tte_add_summary()}/
\code{er_tte_add_model()} instead. This tag is entirely optional; an untagged
custom builder is simply never checked. See \code{\link[=er_style_tag]{er_style_tag()}} for the
full set of attributes a builder can carry.

All five layers are \strong{singleton}: calling the corresponding
\verb{er_tte_add_*()} function again replaces that layer's builder rather than
adding another one. Unlike \code{er_plot()}'s \code{group} layer, no TTE layer is
additive.
}

\section{Passing extra arguments to a builder}{


Every \verb{er_tte_add_*()} function (\code{er_tte_add_curve()},
\code{er_tte_add_censor()}, \code{er_tte_add_risktable()}, \code{er_tte_add_summary()},
\code{er_tte_add_model()}) takes its own \code{...}, forwarded unchanged to \code{style}
when it's actually called at build time. Extra arguments must be named,
since they're appended positionally after the six standard arguments; an
unnamed one errors immediately rather than silently binding to the wrong
parameter. A builder that doesn't need any extra arguments simply
declares \code{...} and ignores it -- every built-in TTE builder does exactly
this.
